From: EglantinePS5
Subject: [PATCH] up: after a removal freed shader programs, RefreshStages looks the stages up again (Lichii #1573), EDEN_UP_SHADER_REMOVAL_REFRESH=1, off by default

What: RemovePendingShaders frees the ShaderInfo of programs whose memory was written, unmapped or
remapped. But RefreshStages returns at once while Dirty::Shaders is clear, so shader_infos keeps
pointing at the freed ShaderInfo until the game writes a shader register again. When a draw then
needs a pipeline that is not built yet, GetGraphicsEnvironments reads the freed memory: a crash in
GetGraphicsEnvironments, or junk read as a shader. This can happen at a scene change, the same
moment as our area-load crashes (those show as out-of-memory in the driver's shader compiler, so
this is not a known cause of them: a guard for a crash at the same moment).

With the switch: a removal that freed programs adds one to a counter (atomic), and RefreshStages
looks the stages up again when it has not seen the latest count. Same code as upstream, behind the
switch. One line at the 1st, 2nd, 4th, 8th, ... such refresh:
"EDEN_UP_SHADER_REMOVAL_REFRESH: stages looked up again after a removal that freed programs (n so far)".

Switch: EDEN_UP_SHADER_REMOVAL_REFRESH=1 (exact "1"), read once. Off: one test of a static bool in
RefreshStages and in RemovePendingShaders, then the old code. Off by default because it can cost a
little: the first draw after such a removal looks up up to 6 programs again, and a program that is
gone is read and hashed again (as when the game writes a shader register).

Test: the log line above shows the case happens on our runs (how often, at which moment). Compare
crash backtraces at area loads (any GetGraphicsEnvironments frame). Watch for a stall at a scene
change; none expected. Not checked: compile and run (upstream also says "not tested at runtime").

Origin and credit: Zortos, zortos293/Lichii 48f1d9b71b (#1573), ported there from
anthonyloukinas/Lichii 730887b (Anthony Loukinas). The upstream text names a related change, #1221,
that makes the case frequent there; we do not have #1221.
---
diff --git a/src/video_core/shader_cache.cpp b/src/video_core/shader_cache.cpp
index fdad74b..ea4aa09 100644
--- a/src/video_core/shader_cache.cpp
+++ b/src/video_core/shader_cache.cpp
@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@
 
 #include <algorithm>
 #include <atomic>
+#include <cstdlib>
 #include <chrono>
 #include <exception>
 #include <cstring>
@@ -156,10 +157,37 @@ u64 ShaderCache::MeshStateHash() const {
     return Common::CityHash64(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(state.data()), sizeof(state)) | 1;
 }
 
+// EDEN_UP_SHADER_REMOVAL_REFRESH=1 (exact "1"; 0746, Lichii #1573): after a removal freed
+// programs, the next RefreshStages looks the stages up again although no shader register was
+// written, so shader_infos never points at a freed ShaderInfo. Off: the old early return.
+[[nodiscard]] static bool ShaderRemovalRefresh() {
+    static const bool enabled = [] {
+        const char* const value = std::getenv("EDEN_UP_SHADER_REMOVAL_REFRESH");
+        return value != nullptr && value[0] == '1' && value[1] == '\0';
+    }();
+    return enabled;
+}
+
 bool ShaderCache::RefreshStages(std::array<u64, 6>& unique_hashes) try {
     auto& dirty{maxwell3d->dirty.flags};
     if (!dirty[VideoCommon::Dirty::Shaders]) {
-        return last_shaders_valid;
+        if (!ShaderRemovalRefresh()) {
+            return last_shaders_valid;
+        }
+        const u64 removals{removal_serial.load(std::memory_order_acquire)};
+        if (removals == seen_removal_serial) {
+            return last_shaders_valid;
+        }
+        // One line at the 1st, 2nd, 4th, 8th, ... time: a removal really left stages to refresh.
+        if (const u64 count{++shader_removal_refreshes}; (count & (count - 1)) == 0) {
+            LOG_INFO(HW_GPU,
+                     "EDEN_UP_SHADER_REMOVAL_REFRESH: stages looked up again after a removal that "
+                     "freed programs ({} so far)",
+                     count);
+        }
+    }
+    if (ShaderRemovalRefresh()) {
+        seen_removal_serial = removal_serial.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
     }
     dirty[VideoCommon::Dirty::Shaders] = false;
 
@@ -422,6 +450,10 @@ void ShaderCache::RemovePendingShaders() {
 
     if (!removed_shaders.empty()) {
         Nx2FastDraw::Break(Nx2FastDraw::Reason::Invalidation);
+        if (ShaderRemovalRefresh()) {
+            // 0746: shader_infos may point at a program freed below.
+            removal_serial.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_release);
+        }
         if (removed_shaders.size() > 16) {
             std::ranges::sort(removed_shaders, std::less<ShaderInfo*>{});
             std::erase_if(storage, [&removed_shaders](const std::unique_ptr<ShaderInfo>& shader) {
diff --git a/src/video_core/shader_cache.h b/src/video_core/shader_cache.h
index bf928ed..d089f79 100644
--- a/src/video_core/shader_cache.h
+++ b/src/video_core/shader_cache.h
@@ -197,6 +197,11 @@ private:
     ::Common::unordered_map<u64, std::vector<Entry*>> invalidation_cache;
     std::vector<std::unique_ptr<ShaderInfo>> storage;
     std::vector<Entry*> marked_for_removal;
+    /// EDEN_UP_SHADER_REMOVAL_REFRESH (0746, Lichii #1573): removals that freed programs, and the
+    /// last one RefreshStages saw; it looks the stages up again after each one.
+    std::atomic<u64> removal_serial{};
+    u64 seen_removal_serial{};
+    u64 shader_removal_refreshes{}; ///< Lookups made only because of a removal (log line)
 
     /// Native programs the lowering rejected, by address, so each draw or dispatch rethrows the
     /// first error instead of lowering again; dropped when guest memory there is written.
